From: infra <infra@localhost>
Date: Mon, 1 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: guard load_evdev_key_for_wayland against None char

On a Wayland session the keymap handler resolves modifiers via
mod_name_to_specific_keysym_name(), which returns None for modifier
names it does not know (e.g. LevelThree from an `lv3:*` XKB option that
maps Left Alt to Mod5). That None is then fed straight into
xkb.keysym_from_name(char), which calls char.encode('ascii') and raises
AttributeError, killing the dialpad daemon on connect.

Callers already treat a falsy return as "no evdev key" (e.g.
`if not mod_as_evdev_key: continue`), and the function already returns
None implicitly when nothing matches, so returning None early for an
unresolvable char is consistent and non-fatal.
--- a/dialpad.py
+++ b/dialpad.py
@@ -1779,6 +1779,9 @@
 def load_evdev_key_for_wayland(char, keyboard_state):
     global gnome_current_layout_index
 
+    if char is None:
+        return None
+
     keysym = xkb.keysym_from_name(char)
 
     keymap = keyboard_state.get_keymap()
